            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0429" n="329" xml:id="pg329"/>

                <head>No. 231.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr. Langston.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-10-01"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 1,
                        1884</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 300.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Mr. W. K. Yan Bokkelen, father of Mr. G. A.
                    Yan Bokkelen, whose arrest and imprisonment at Port au Prince for debt has been
                    the subject of recent correspondence with your legation, naturally shows the
                    greatest solicitude for his son, and frequently addresses the Department in his
                    behalf, while your No. 647 of July 7 last, in giving a detailed statement of the
                    case, said that Mr. Yan Bokkelen’s health was not very good, and that in
                    consequence thereof you had succeeded in having him removed from the jail to the
                    military hospital.</p>
                <p>I am constrained, therefore, to inquire whether the appeal foreshadowed in your
                    No. 647 has been taken, and, if so, whether the decision of the court of
                    cassation has been rendered. If not, and Mr. Yan Bokkelen is still in
                    confinement, you will take every proper step to obtain his immediate
                    release.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
